{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why this exists\n\n`src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py` is at **exactly 800 lines** — the hard cap. Sibling\n`elfin-decagonal-polarbear` (F11, mirror F11) must add a `warn_cross_session` flag to `Route`\nand fold it into `derive_policy_sets`, which cannot be done without going over.\n\nThis is filed as its own ticket deliberately. Splitting a module is a structural change with\nits own review surface; folding it into a mirror-remediation change would put an unrelated\nrefactor inside a change whose reviewed plan is about single-sourcing a policy set, and would\nmake both harder to review and to revert.\n\n**This has already bitten this epic twice.** `_commands/composer.py` was likewise at exactly\n800; the F3 change added an import and a comment, took the whole stack to `Verified -1`, and\nthe recovery mis-amended a commit and opened a duplicate Gerrit change. F7 then had to express\nits fix in ZERO net lines to fit. That is code shaped around a cap rather than around meaning.\n\nFour other `src/rebar` files sit at exactly 800 today (`llm/plan_review/sizing.py`,\n`llm/plan_review/sidecar.py`, `_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py`,\n`_commands/composer.py`), so this is a general hazard; this ticket addresses only the one the\nepic is blocked on.\n\n## The seam\n\nNot a mechanical cut to hit a number. `_registry.py` holds three clusters, and one is already\nself-contained:\n\n- **route TABLE construction** — `_core_factory`, `_reads_init_only`, `_simple_read_groups`,\n  `_lifecycle`, `_writes_full`, `_intercepts`, `_bridge_and_arms`, `_build_routes` (~520 lines)\n- **types + lookup** — `Route`, `Finding`, `_index`, `route_for`, `derive_policy_sets`\n- **registry VALIDATION** (`:684-795`, ~112 lines) — `_is_valid_reference`, the six `_check_*`\n  functions, `_CHECKS`, `validate`\n\nThe validation cluster is the seam: its functions call only each other, take\n`routes: tuple[Route, ...]` as a PARAMETER, and yield `Finding`. It reads the registry; it does\nnot build it. Extracting it leaves `_registry.py` at roughly 688 with ~112 lines of headroom,\nand the new module lands well above the 100-LOC floor, so this is a split along an existing\ncall-graph seam rather than one invented for the cap.\n\nThe consumer surface is verified rather than assumed. 22 files reference\n`rebar._cli._registry`, and across named imports and module-handle access they consume exactly\neight names: `ROUTES`, `Route`, `validate`, `route_for`, `derive_policy_sets`, `ADAPTER_KINDS`,\n`INIT_POLICIES`, `KNOWN_CAPABILITIES`. Of the cluster being moved, only `validate` is consumed\nfrom outside, so `validate` must stay importable from `rebar._cli._registry`. `Finding` is\nconsumed NOWHERE outside the module — it is the validation cluster's own return type, named\nonly by the `_check_*` functions that move with it — so it needs no re-export. (`test_capabilities`\nand `test_cli_registry` import `Route, validate`; `test_cli_registry_dispatch` imports the module\nhandle. None of the three imports `Finding`.) The in-tree precedent for the back-compat\nre-export is `gate_dispatch.py`, which re-exports the `plan_review_recovery` cluster and\ndocuments the re-export as load-bearing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: the validation cluster moves to a new `src/rebar/_cli/_registry_checks.py`, and\n      `_registry.py` is under the cap with real headroom. Verified by `wc -l` on both and by the\n      CI module-size gate passing.\n- [ ] AC2: the eight externally-consumed names remain resolvable on `rebar._cli._registry`\n      after the split. Verified by a test that asserts each of `ROUTES`, `Route`, `validate`,\n      `route_for`, `derive_policy_sets`, `ADAPTER_KINDS`, `INIT_POLICIES` and\n      `KNOWN_CAPABILITIES` is present on the module, and by the existing consumer suites\n      (`test_capabilities`, `test_cli_registry`, `test_cli_registry_dispatch`) passing\n      untouched.\n- [ ] AC3: behavior is byte-identical — the same findings, in the same order, for the same\n      routes. Verified by a test asserting `tuple(validate(ROUTES))` is unchanged and by\n      `validate()` still returning empty on the committed registry.\n- [ ] AC4: the new module is above the 100-LOC floor, so this is a seam extraction and not a\n      fragment. Verified by `wc -l`.\n- [ ] AC5: no route definition, alias, or policy set is altered. Verified by a test asserting\n      the `ROUTES` name/alias sets and every `derive_policy_sets` output are identical to\n      before, so the split cannot have moved behavior.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit -k \"registry or capabilities or dispatch or cli\" -q` — AC2, AC3,\n  AC5 and the existing registry suites.\n- `wc -l src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py src/rebar/_cli/_registry_checks.py` — AC1, AC4.\n- `make lint && make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788400236922435001,"uuid":"8a4d9716-e311-4c06-8dd1-284e9d669c74"}